## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Studies of the interaction between polyelectrolytes and oppositely charged surfactants (PSC) in aqueous solutions constitute now a wide area of modern colloid science. Various aspects of polyelectrolyte– surfactant interactions are considered in numerous reviews [1–14]. This interest is caused by PSC applications in different branches of industry and medicine, for example, in oil recovery, pharmaceutical formulations, film coating, for drug and gene delivery. Besides, there is also a fundamental interest in polymer interactions with molecules of low molecular weight and in intermolecular association phenomena. If the first studies dealt mainly with dilute PSC solutions [1–5,15,16], since the middle of the nineties the solid state of PSC has also attracted growing attention [6,10,17].
Although the surface activity of the complexes is usually extremely high and the surface properties of their solutions are also crucial for technological applications, surface tension measurements have practically been the only experimental technique applied to mixed polyelectrolyte/surfactant adsorption layers at the liquid–gas interface until the last decade [3–5,15,16]. The complex formation in the surface layer is evidenced by the surface tension decrease observed for polyelectrolyte/surfactant mixtures at such concentrations where the polyelectrolyte or surfactant alone does not change the surface properties of water. Most of the authors measured the surface tension of aqueous PSC solutions as a function of surfactant concentration at constant polyelectrolyte content. The surface tension decreased with increasing surfactant concentration up to a critical value where the rate of decrease dropped abruptly. One can connect this critical concentration with the onset of aggregate formation in the bulk [5,13,18]. However, some potentiometric studies showed that the critical aggregation concentration (CAC) can be lower than the concentration range in which abrupt changes in the surface tension isotherms are observed [11,19,20]. Another important peculiarity of the surface tension dependence on surfactant concentration consists in a strong local maximum (“cliff edge peak”) in the range beyond the CAC for some PSC solutions, for example, of poly(diallyldimethylammonium chloride)/sodium dodecylsulfate (PDADMAC/SDS) [21–24], sodium poly(styrenesulfonate)/alkyltrimethylammonium bromides $(\mathrm{PSS}/\mathrm{C_{n}T A B})$ [26], poly(vinylpyridinium) chloride/SDS [27] (PVPCl/ SDS) and poly(ethyleneimine)/SDS (PEI/SDS) [28]. Possible explanations of this effect have been widely discussed recently. Taylor et al. assumed that apart from the complexes of high surface activity some non-adsorbing PSC are also formed at higher surfactant concentrations [8,26,27]. The competition between these two kinds of complexes results in the “cliff edge peak” of the surface tension isotherm close to the concentration ${\sf C}_{0}$ where the amount of the surfactant ion charge compensates the opposite charge of the polymer in bulk. Bell et al. developed these ideas further and constructed an equilibrium thermodynamic model to describe the surface tension isotherm [29,30]. However, recent experimental studies discovered the non-equilibrium nature of the local surface tension maximum of PSC solutions. Campbell et al. devoted their paper to the dynamic surface properties of PDADMAC/SDS solutions and noted that the surface concentrations depend strongly on the composition of slowly diffusing macroscopic aggregates which are formed in the system close to ${\sf C}_{0}$ . [23]. It was shown in Ref. [24] that PDADMAC/SDS solutions without inert electrolyte displayed the strong “cliff edge peak” at surface ages of several hours but the effect disappeared if one waited a few times longer. At last Campbell et al. have demonstrated recently that the “cliff edge peak” for PDADMACC/SDS solutions in $0.1\mathrm{~M~NaCl}$ can disappear as a result of agitation and be produced again after aging without any mechanical disturbances [25].
The available information on the surface structure of PSC solutions has increased significantly during the last decade as a result of the application of different experimental techniques, including X-ray [31–34] reflectivity, X-ray diffraction [34], ellipsometry [19,23,35–39],
Brewster angle microscopy [32,33,39,40], surface shear rheology [38,41]. Among the applied methods the neutron reflectivity provided the most detailed data on the mean adsorption layer thickness, the adsorbed amount, and unique information on the polyelectrolyte and surfactant distribution normal to the interface, [8,18,21,22,26–28,42]. At the same time the use of this technique is limited to short time intervals and the relatively large size of the neutron beam does not allow investigation of the two-dimensional structure of the adsorption layers. These deficiencies inherent to the most powerful experimental technique are probably the main cause of the insufficient attention to the non-equilibrium states of the surface layers of PSC solutions until recently (cf. the interpretation of the “cliff edge peak” above). On the other hand, the differences in the size between neutron and light beams in the neutron reflectivity and ellipsometry probably resulted in the difficulties to give concerted interpretation of the experimental data of these two methods for PSS/dodecyltrimethylammonium bromide (PSS/DTAB) solutions [19].
It is noteworthy that the non-equilibrium nature of the bulk dispersions of polyelectrolyte/surfactant aggregates has also been frequently underestimated. One of the achievements of the last decade consists in the discovery that the aggregate size in PSC solutions depends on the mixing protocol, the true equilibrium is approached extremely slow and one usually deals with trapped longlived metastable states [13,43–45]. It is usually deemed that the mixing protocol influences the surface properties of these systems to a less extent [11,13,44]. However recent studies showed that the history of the system under investigation can have crucial influence on the system response to a small contraction (expansion) of the surface area or to the approach of the surface properties to equilibrium values if the solution bulk contains macroscopic aggregates, or the adsorption layer behaves like a two-dimensional solid phase [11,23–25,40,46]. In this case one can achieve deeper insight into the surface structure by measuring the kinetic dependencies of the surface properties and studying consecutive steps of the adsorption layer formation [24,47–52]. Measurements of the dynamic dilational surface elasticity proved to be especially useful for these purposes. Unlike kinetic dependencies of the surface tension or the adsorbed amount, the dynamic surface elasticity of polymer systems as a function of surface age can have a few local maxima corresponding to different conformational transitions in the surface layer [12,24,47–51]. The early studies on the dilational surface rheology of PSC solutions usually did not take into account these peculiarities and the authors measured the dynamic surface elasticity close to equilibrium as a function of concentration and frequency [32,33,53,54].
Recent application of the dilational surface rheology to PSC solutions has allowed also the discovery of surface heterogeneities in these systems. The formation of microparticles in the surface layer or their adsorption from solution can result in a strong drop of the dynamic surface elasticity, thereby leading to non-monotonous kinetic and concentration dependencies of this quantity [12,24,47–52,55]. One can obtain additional information on these microparticles at the liquid–gas interface from ellipsometric measurements [19,24,46], scanning atomic force microscopy, if the adsorption layer is transferred to a mica surface by the Langmuir–Blodgett method [56], or from the non-linear response of the system to small expansion and compression of the liquid surface [50,51]. The obtained results show that the microheterogeneous liquid surfaces are more widespread than one could assume earlier. This can result in the reconsideration of some principles of the dilational surface rheology and have impact on areas of colloid science where the surface dilational rheological properties are especially important, like the investigation of foam and emulsion formation and stability.
The main aim of this work is to give a review of recent results on microheterogeneous adsorption layers of complexes between synthetic polyelectrolytes and oppositely charged conventional surfactants, and to delineate the advantages of the dilational surface rheology in the application to these systems. After a brief survey of the theoretical models of the dilational surface rheology of complex fluids, suitable experimental methods are described. The main section of the given work contains a review of recent experimental results on the dilational rheology of PSC adsorption layers with emphasis on systems containing microparticles. The conclusions from these results are presented in the last section which indicates also the perspectives of future studies.
## 2. Dynamic dilational surface elasticity of complex fluids
It seems counterintuitive at first sight that the surface of transparent solution can contain some spots where the surface properties differ from those of the neighboring area (two-dimensional aggregates). Over a long period of time it was considered surfactant adsorption layers (Gibbs surface layers) to be different in this respect from spread insoluble monolayers (Langmuir monolayers) where two-dimensional phase transitions had been discovered about a century ago resulting in the surface heterogeneity in some ranges of the surface concentration [57]. This was probably one of the reasons why different models of dilational surface rheology were applied to Gibbs and Langmuir monolayers. In the latter case the experimental methods of dilational surface rheology gave a possibility to estimate the main scale of the surface heterogeneity [58]. The discovery of twodimensional phase transitions in adsorption layers in the nineties [59,60] changed the traditional point of view but was not accompanied by appreciable progress in the surface dilational rheology of heterogeneous surface layers [61].
The system response to a small expansion or compression of the liquid surface is determined by the dynamic dilational surface elasticity E which is the ratio of the surface tension increment $\updelta\upgamma$ and the corresponding increment of the relative surface area δ(lnS). This is a complex quantity and depends on the angular frequency ω of surface perturbations. The first and up to now most popular expression for E relates to the case of a surfactant adsorption layer with a purely diffusion controlled adsorption mechanism [62]
$$
E\equiv\frac{\updelta\gamma}{\updelta l n S}=E_{0}[1+(1-i)\zeta]^{-1}=E_{0}\frac{1+\zeta+i\zeta}{1+2\zeta+2\zeta^{2}}
$$
where the high frequency elasticity limit $\mathtt{E}_{0}$ , the characteristic frequency ${\displaystyle\mathfrak{o}}_{\mathrm{D}}$ , and the parameter $\zeta$ are given by
$$
E_{0}=-\frac{d\gamma}{d l n\Gamma},\upomega_{D}=\frac{D}{2}\left(\frac{d c}{d\Gamma}\right)^{2},\upzeta=\left(\frac{\upomega_{D}}{\upomega}\right)^{1/2}.
$$
These parameters are determined by the surfactant's diffusion coefficient D and the equilibrium dependencies of surface tension on the adsorbed amount $\upgamma(\Gamma)$ , and the adsorbed amount in turn on surfactant concentration $\Gamma({\mathsf{c}})$ .
This approach can be easily extended to the case of the mixed adsorption mechanism [63]
$$
E=E_{0}\frac{i\upomega\uptau+\left(1+i\right)/2\upzeta}{1+i\upomega\uptau+\left(1+i\right)/2\upzeta}
$$
where $\tau=(\alpha_{k}+\beta_{k}c/{Gamma_{\infty}})^{-1}$ is the relaxation time of the proper adsorption process, which for a Langmuir adsorption kinetics is determined by the kinetic coefficients of adsorption and desorption, $\upalpha_{\mathrm{k}}$ and $\upbeta_{\mathrm{k}},$ respectively, and by the limiting adsorption $\Gamma_{\infty}$ .
The dilational surface elasticity of micellar solutions can be described by more cumbersome expressions [64]. In this case one has to consider separately the time scales corresponding to the fast and slow steps of micellar formation (disintegration). If the number of soluble surfactants exceeds one, the theory complicates to a greater extent. In this case, an exact solution was obtained only for a mixture of two surfactants under the condition of a diffusional exchange between the surface layer and the bulk phase [65]. A simple relation for an arbitrary number N of surfactants arises only in the limit of small surface coverage [63]
$$
E=E_{T0}-\sum_{j}^{N}\frac{\updelta_{j}}{1+i\upomega\uptau_{j}+\left(1+i\right)/2\zeta_{j}}
$$
where the subscript $\mathrm{~j~}$ indicates that the corresponding quantity corresponds to the surfactant j, $\mathtt{E}_{\mathrm{T}0}$ is the high frequency value of the dynamic surface elasticity when there is no matter exchange between surface layer and bulk phase, and it reduces to $\mathtt{E}_{0}$ in case of a single surfactant, $\updelta_{\mathrm{j}}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{T0}}-(\updelta\upgamma/\updelta\mathrm{ln}\mathsf{S})_{\Gamma,\mathrm{k}\neq\mathrm{j}}$ is the surface relaxation strength, the superscript at the derivative means that only surfactant j can participate in the matter exchange between the surface layer and the bulk phase.
There is another important limiting case when one can obtain a relatively concise expression of the dynamic surface elasticity for an arbitrary number of surfactants. If all the relaxation processes occur inside the surface layer, the surface elasticity E becomes a twodimensional analog of the bulk dilational dynamic elasticity. The latter quantity plays the main role in the ultrasound spectroscopy and was investigated in detail in [66,67]. The formal techniques developed for the chemical relaxation in the bulk phase can be also applied to the surface dilational elasticity. Almost all the relaxation processes in the system can be treated formally as chemical reactions and thereby one can use the methods of linear algebra. They allow formally to come from a number of real processes in the surface layer to a system of “normal chemical reactions” when all the matrices of thermodynamic derivatives and kinetic coefficients become simultaneously diagonal. This approach gives the following expression of the dynamic surface elasticity [63]
$$
\varepsilon\equiv\frac{\delta\gamma}{\delta\ln S}=\left(\frac{\partial\gamma}{\partial\ln S}\right)_{\xi}-\sum_{j=1}^{N}\frac{\left(\frac{\partial\gamma}{\partial\ln S}\right)_{\xi}-\left(\frac{\partial\gamma}{\partial\ln S}\right)_{A_{k\neq j},\xi_{j}}}{1+\mathrm{i}\upomega\tau_{\mathrm{j}}}
$$
where $\tau_{\mathrm{j}}$ is the isothermic relaxation time of the normal process $\mathrm{j}$ in the surface layer, $\xi_{\mathrm{j}}$ is the corresponding chemical variable (or a structural parameter for processes different from the chemical reactions), $\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{j}}$ is the chemical affinity. The subscript $\xi_{\mathrm{j}}$ means that the derivative corresponds to a non-equilibrium process (the process j is frozen) but is taken at the equilibrium values of the thermodynamic variables. The subscript $\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{k}}$ indicates the chemical equilibrium for the process k.
The key point at the application of this approach is the transition to “normal reactions”. Some approximations to describe the dilational surface elasticity of a surface layer with chemical reactions were also proposed in [68,69].
Eq. (5) can be applied to different relaxation processes in the surface layer, for example, to molecular reorientations or to the exchange of polymer segments between the proximal and distal (the region of tails and loops) regions of the polymer adsorption layer. In the latter case one can connect the relaxation times with the static and dynamic properties of a polymer chain [70,71]. The surface tension of polymers solutions depends first of all on the monomer concentration in the proximal region and not on the concentration in the distal region. Then the relaxation of surface stresses to a surface dilation proceeds at the expense of drawing chains up to the surface or squeezing chains out of it. In the case of small deformations the departure of a polymer coil as a whole from the surface or the reverse process of entanglement of a free coil to the surface turns out to be relatively rare events and one can neglect their influence. In this model the exchange of monomers between the proximal and distal regions under expansion (compression) of the surface can take place as a result of two processes: the relaxation of inner strains of a polymer chain or the drawing up (squeezing in) of the chain as a whole. The first process can be described mathematically by means of a solution of the corresponding boundary problem for the Rouse equation, while the second process can be considered in the framework of the two-dimensional reptation model proposed by de Gennes.
These simple ideas lead to the following expression for the dynamic surface elasticity [70]
$$
\varepsilon=-\frac{\partial\gamma}{\partial\ln\Gamma_{T}}\left[\frac{2\Gamma_{3}}{\uppi\Gamma_{T}}\upSigma^{\prime}\frac{8\mathrm{i}\uptau_{\mathrm{B}}\upomega}{\uprho^{2}\uppi^{2}\left(1+\mathrm{i}\upomega\uptau_{\mathrm{B}}/\up p^{2}\right)}+\left(1-\frac{2\Gamma_{3}}{\uppi\Gamma_{T}}\right)\frac{\mathrm{i}\upomega\uptau_{2}}{1+\mathrm{i}\upomega\uptau_{2}}\right]
$$
where p changes from 1 to infinity and the sum includes only the odd terms, $\Gamma_{\mathrm{T}}$ is the number of moles of monomers per surface element of unit area in the proximal region, $\Gamma_{3}$ is the number of moles of those monomers per unit area that belong to trains in the proximal region restricted by two loops or tails, $\tau_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the relaxation time of the inner stresses of a polymer chain
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{B}}=\frac{b^{2}N_{0}^{2}}{3\uppi^{2}k_{B}T B}.
$$
The relaxation time $\tau_{2}$ corresponds to the motion of a train in the proximal region as a single entity
$$
\uptau_{2}=-\frac{\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}}{\mathrm{B}\Gamma_{2}\mathbf{a}(\partial\gamma/\partial\Gamma_{T})}.
$$
$\Gamma_{2}$ is the number of moles of monomers per unit area in the proximal region, which belong to trains restricted by a loop or tail only from one side (polymer tails lying on the surface), $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ is the mean number of monomers in such a train, $\Nu_{0}$ is the number of monomers inside the two-dimensional tube of entanglements, b is the statistical segment length, a is the mean distance between two neighboring entanglements forming the two-dimensional tube, B is the mobility constant, T is the temperature, and $\bf k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant.
This model describes the dynamic surface properties of non-ionic homopolymer solutions but it can be also applied to polyelectrolyte solutions if the addition inorganic salt results in the shielding of electrostatic interactions between the segments.
In the limiting case when the concentration in the bulk phase tends to zero and the interaction of monomers with the surface is strong enough, the adsorbed chain does not form loops and tails, $\Gamma_{2}$ tends to zero and $\tau_{2}$ to infinity. Then
$$
\varepsilon=-\frac{\partial\gamma}{\partial\ln\Gamma}
$$
and the adsorbed film becomes purely elastic.
The reverse limiting case of high bulk concentration is more complicated. Then the number of loops increases, $\tau_{2}$ and $\tau_{\mathrm{B}}$ tend to zero and one can expect that the surface elasticity tends to zero too. However, the polymer chains can interact in the distal region of the surface layer. In this case the global reorganization of the surface layer structure under dilation can lead to a small but finite dynamic surface elasticity [72].
There are also some special cases when Eq. (5) can be applied to a heterogeneous surface layer. If the only relaxation process is the matter exchange between two coexisting surface phases in a monolayer, Eq. (5) yields for each of the phases
$$
E^{j}=E_{0}^{j}{\frac{i\omega/r^{j}}{1+i\upomega/r^{j}}}
$$
where the superscript $\mathbf{j}=1$ , 2 indicates the phase and ${\mathrm{\bf{r}}}^{\mathrm{j}}$ is the kinetic coefficient of the surfactant transition from the phase $\mathrm{j}$ to another two-dimensional phase.
Note that Eq. (10) coincides with the complex version of Maxwell's rheological model and can be also applied to a continuous twodimensional phase coexisting with a number of three-dimensional aggregates in the adsorption layer, if they occupy a negligible surface area. Veer and van den Tempel derived a more general relation for the latter case taking into account the matter exchange between the surface layer and the bulk phase [73]
$$
E=E_{0}\frac{i}{r/\upomega+\upzeta+i(1+\upzeta)}
$$
It coincides with Eq. (10) if $r{>}{>}\zeta\omega$ .
In real experiments one usually does not measure the dynamic surface elasticity of separate phases but the global dilational elasticity of the whole interface. In this case one can apply Eqs. (10, 11) only if the area fraction of one of the phases is negligible $\mathrm{F}^{1}\equiv S^{1}/S^{2}\ll1$ . Then $\mathtt{E}_{0}$ equals the high frequency surface elasticity of the phase 1(E01).
To the best of our knowledge only Lucassen considered the more general case of finite areas of all the coexisting surface phases or particles floating at the interface and obtained the following relationship between the global dilational elasticity of the interface E and local elasticities of coexisting surface phases $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{E}}^{\mathrm{j}}$ [74]
$$
{\frac{1}{E}}={\frac{F^{1}}{E^{1}}}+{\frac{F^{2}}{E^{2}}}+...+{\frac{F^{n}}{E^{n}}}=\sum_{i}^{n}{\frac{F^{i}}{E^{i}}}
$$
where $\mathrm{F^{i}}$ represents the area fraction of the surface phase j.
Eq. (12) is a rather general one and can be applied to heterogeneous interfaces of different nature. In the simplest case of floating incompressible particles, which do not change their shape and the occupied area at any variations of the tension of the adjoining fluid–fluid interface, the global surface elasticity equals the elasticity of free interface divided by the fraction of the free interface. This result shows that the simple excluded area effect can strongly increase the measured dilational surface elasticity.
In general the surface compression or expansion in the system containing surfactants together with floating particles results in changes of the contact angle θ and thereby of the area occupied by the particles. Lucassen considered in detail some particular cases of floating solid monodisperse particles and drops, for example, spherical particle at the liquid–air interface under the condition of adsorption equilibrium at all interfaces [74]. In this case the application of Young's law and Gibbs adsorption equation leads to the following expression for the dilational surface elasticity
$$
E=\frac{E_{L A}}{1+n\uppi R^{2}\left[\frac{2E_{L A}c o s^{2}\uptheta}{\upgamma_{L A}}\left(1-\frac{\upalpha}{c o s\uptheta}\right)-s i n^{2}\uptheta\right]}
$$
where $\mathtt{E}_{\mathrm{LA}}$ and $\upgamma_{\mathrm{LA}}$ are the dilational elasticity and tension of the liquid–air interface, respectively, n is the number of particles per unit surface area, R is the particle radius, and the ratio $\upalpha\equiv(\Gamma_{\mathrm{SA}}-\Gamma_{\mathrm{SL}})/\Gamma_{\mathrm{LA}}$ includes the adsorbed amounts $\Gamma_{\mathrm{SA}},\Gamma_{\mathrm{SL}},$ and $\Gamma_{\mathrm{LA}}$ at the solid–air, solid– liquid, and liquid–air interfaces.
The global surface elasticity depends strongly on the ratio $\mathrm{E_{LA}}/\upgamma_{\mathrm{LA}}$ and in some ranges of the parameters it can have negative values. This peculiarity can arise only for heterogeneous interfaces and never occurs if the surface is uniform and the system is close to equilibrium.
The results of Lucassen [74] show that the surface heterogeneity has a strong effect on the dynamic surface properties. Unfortunately, one can calculate the corresponding changes of the dilational surface elasticity only for a limited number of systems when the floating particles have well defined shape and properties (contact angle, particle–liquid and particle–air surface tensions). The determination of these parameters is hardly possible for polyelectrolyte/surfactant aggregates at the solution–air interface. Moreover, the transitional region between the nano-sized aggregates and the surrounding adsorption layer when segments of the same macromolecule can belong to both interfacial regions is not so well defined as for interfacial layers between two macroscopic phases. These features of the system under investigation do not allow the application of the derived exact mathematical relations. Only some qualitative conclusions of the theoretical investigation can be useful.
Special difficulties arise if one studies the visco-elasticity of a heterogeneous adsorption layer by the experimental methods based on the propagation of capillary waves. The use of the classical dispersion relation of capillary waves in this case can lead to significant errors due to the wave scattering by small aggregates [75]. The scattering results in an additional contribution to the measured effective wave damping coefficient. If the aggregate concentration increases, multiple scattering effects have to be taken into account. Although the theory of this phenomenon is rather complicated it results in the following simple relation for the effective damping coefficient (the imaginary part of the measured complex wave number K) [76]
$$
\mathrm Ḋ \mathrm Ḋ K Ḍ Ḍ =\mathrm{Im}k+n Q_{s}/2
$$
where $\mathrm{\Deltak}$ is the complex wave number for the homogeneous surface without aggregates, $\mathsf{Q}_{s}$ is the total scattering length of the aggregate and $\mathfrak{n}$ is the number of particles (aggregates) per unit surface area.
The calculations of the total scattering length on the basis of the properties of microparticles show that the influence of scattering is small and usually negligible if the dynamic surface elasticity of surface aggregates (surface dispersion particles) is comparable or higher than the dynamic elasticity of the surface dispersion phase [75]. A special situation arises when the continuous surface layer contains regions of a two-dimensional gaseous phase (two-dimensional bubbles). If the dynamic surface elasticity of the scatterer tends to zero, multiple scattering effects become noticeable [76].
## 3. Experimental methods
All methods of surface rheology are based on a perturbation of the mechanical equilibrium at the interface and the subsequent measurement of the system response. If the perturbation can be reduced to a small change of the surface area, this response is determined by a fundamental surface property—the complex dynamic dilational surface elasticity E. The existing experimental techniques allow to measure this quantity up to frequencies of about 2 MHz [12,77]. The low limit of the frequency range is less certain. The modern electromechanical devices do not limit the frequency from below. In this case the frequency limit is mainly determined by the patience of the experimentalist. The number of proposed methods exceeds ten but only a few of them were applied to PSC solutions. All the existing experimental techniques at high frequencies $(>500\mathrm{Hz})$ are based on the measurements of the characteristics of capillary waves. Adsorbed PSC layers behave like insoluble monolayers in this frequency range and the dynamic surface elasticity is determined mainly by the interactions between adsorbed molecules and complexes. Nowadays the experimental techniques based on capillary wave methods are not as popular as they were in the nineties [77–79] and were applied to PSC solutions only a few times [32,33,54]. Most of the authors prefer to use low frequency surface rheological methods $\left(\mathsf{f}{<}1\mathsf{H z}\right)$ . This is probably caused by a significant decrease of the accuracy of capillary wave methods if ${\mathrm{E}}{>}0.16{\upgamma}$ . This condition complicates the application of capillary wave techniques to highly elastic PSC adsorption films [77].
One can divide the experimental techniques at low frequencies $(<1\mathsf{H z})$ into two groups in accordance with the shape of the liquid interface under investigation. The use of curved liquid meniscuses in the oscillating bubble and drop methods has an obvious advantage. In this case one can study small liquid volumes up to a few microliters. The commercial availability of the corresponding instruments made these two methods currently the most popular [11,24,38,40,80–86]. Unfortunately, the oscillating bubble and drop methods do not ensure uniform surface dilation. If the surface tension oscillations are determined by profile analysis tensiometry, also shear surface properties must be taken into account [87]. Moreover, the shear surface viscosity can lead to an apparent “Maxwellian behavior” of the effective complex surface elasticity and thereby mimic a lowfrequency relaxation process for a pure elastic adsorption layer. Another problem stems out from the strong violation of the mechanical equilibrium close to the interface with increasing the oscillation frequency [88]. The oscillation can have non-radial components and one cannot describe the meniscus shape by a simple Young–Laplace equation. This limits from above the frequency range accessible to the oscillating drop method. If one applies profile analysis tensiometry, the limiting frequency is less than $1\mathrm{Hz}$ [88] but the limit depends on the properties of the system, the operation of the source of mechanical oscillations and can be lower for oscillating bubbles. Besides, it is seldom taken into account that the three phase contact line can also influence the meniscus oscillations [24]. This effect can explain the fact that the material of the capillary can influence the surface elasticity measured by the oscillating bubble and drop methods for some systems.
The results under discussion in this work were mainly obtained by two experimental methods based on sinusoidal compression (expansion) of almost flat liquid surfaces. Although these techniques require larger quantities of the investigated solution, they seem to be more reliable because of the less complicated hydrodynamics and the use of lower relative oscillation amplitudes. The latter condition is especially important for heterogeneous PSC adsorption layers which exhibit a non-linear response even at surface area changes of about few percents [40]. In the case of a flat liquid surface one can induce almost uniform surface compression and expansion in the center of a Langmuir trough using barrier or ring oscillations at low frequencies $(<0.2\:\mathrm{Hz})$ . These two experimental methods are discussed below in some more details. In addition, the experimental data are compared with those obtained from oscillating drop and bubble experiments so that this technique is also briefly presented.
The most of investigated adsorption films are elastic. The real part of the surface elasticity is much higher than the imaginary part and thereby almost coincides with the elasticity modulus. However, this is not a general rule. These two parts can have close values in some concentration ranges, for example, in the case of PVPCl/SDS solutions where the imaginary part can even exceed the real one (cf. Section 4.4 below).
## 3.1. Oscillating barrier method
The oscillations of surface tension in the case of the oscillating barrier and ring methods are both the result of harmonic expansions and compressions of an almost flat liquid surface at a given amplitude. The real $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{r}}$ and imaginary $\mathrm{E_{i}}$ components of the complex dynamic surface elasticity can be calculated from the results of simultaneous measurements of the amplitudes of the surface tension $\updelta\upgamma$ and surface area δS, and the phase shift $\boldsymbol{\Phi}$ between the oscillations of these two quantities according to the following relation
$$
E=E_{r}+i E_{i}=\frac{S\S\gamma}{\delta S}\ c o s\varphi+i\frac{S\delta\gamma}{\delta S}\ s i n\varphi.
$$
The difference between the two experimental techniques consists mainly in the way of surface deformation. Fig. 1 shows a scheme of the experimental set-up for the measurement of the dynamic surface elasticity by the oscillating barrier method [55,71].
A mechanical generator of low frequency $(0.001\mathrm{-}0.2\:\mathrm{Hz}^{\cdot}$ ) oscillations transforms the rotation of an electromotor to a translational motion with reversion and gives the possibility to control the frequency and amplitude. The moving part of the generator is connected to a Teflon barrier by a hollow steel rod. A micrometer connected with the steel rod gives the possibility to determine the amplitude of barrier oscillations and thereby the amplitude of surface area oscillations. In operation the barrier glides back and forth along the polished brims of the Langmuir trough and produces the harmonic surface area oscillations of the liquid surface in the trough. The amplitude of the barrier oscillations can be varied between 0.4 and $4\%$ . The moment of maximum compression of the liquid surface in the Langmuir through is fixed by a photodetector to determine the phase shift between the surface tension and surface area oscillations.
The induced oscillations of the surface tension are determined by the Wilhelmy plate method using an electronic microbalance. A rectangular glass plate $(20\times20\times0.25\mathrm{mm})$ was used for this purpose and its surfaces were sandblasted to ensure complete wetting. Before measurements the plate was washed by a freshly prepared chromic acid mixture and after that by triple-distilled water, and dried. When the plate touches the liquid surface the resulting force can be determined by the microbalance with an accuracy of $\pm0.1\mathrm{mN}$ .
The application of the oscillating barrier method is possible only at the oscillation frequencies less than approximately $0.2\mathrm{Hz}$ when the length of surface longitudinal waves far exceeds the length of the Langmuir trough and the surface tension oscillations in the trough are homogeneous.
## 3.2. Oscillating ring method
The dilational surface elasticity of PSC solutions were also measured by the oscillating ring method proposed firstly by Kokelaar et al. [89]. The surface tension oscillations were measured by the Wilhelmy plate like in the case of the oscillating barrier method using a similar glass plate and electronic balance.
The essential part of the used experimental set-up (Fig. 2) was a cylindrical glass ring of the diameter of $7\mathrm{cm}$ and the height of $2.5\mathrm{cm}$ . It had a sandblasted inner surface to ensure complete wetting and was used for periodic expansion and compression of the interface under investigation. The ring was placed with its main axis perpendicular to the liquid surface and partially immersed into the liquid in a glass Petri dish. The oscillations of the ring along its axis resulted in the oscillations of the liquid surface area inside the ring due to periodic changes of the meniscus height and shape at the internal ring surface.
To induce the ring oscillations the rotational motion of a disk with an eccentric wheel produced by an electromotor was transformed into the translational motion with reversion. The relative amplitude of oscillations could be changed from 0.7 to $7\%$ . The phase difference between the oscillations of the ring and thereby of the liquid surface area inside the ring and the surface tension oscillations measured by the Wilhelmy plate inside the ring were determined using a photodetector. This device produced an electric signal when the ring took the highest position and the surface area was maximum. The Petri dish was situated in a closed chamber for the minimization of air flow near the liquid surface.
The main advantage of the oscillating ring technique consists in almost pure dilational deformations of the liquid surface and thereby in a negligible contribution of shear stresses to experimental results.
The dynamic surface elasticity of insoluble monolayers of polyethylene oxide (PEO) and octadecylmethyl phosphine oxide (ODPO) was measured by the oscillating barrier and oscillating ring method to calibrate the experimental set-up in Fig. 2. PEO and ODPO monolayers at the water surface were almost purely elastic $\mathrm{(E_{r}\gg E_{i})}$ and characterized by different values of the surface elasticity [72,90]. The dependence of the dynamic surface elasticity of PEO monolayers on the area per molecule had a maximum of $12\mathrm{mN/m}$ . ODPO monolayers gave the possibility to obtain higher values of the surface elasticity up to $50\mathrm{mN/m}$ . The calibration procedure resulted in a linear relationship between the signal of the oscillating ring method and the surface elasticity measured by the oscillating barrier method for a given oscillation amplitude, and allowed calculation of the relative surface area change inside the ring.
## 3.3. Oscillating drop and bubble method
In recent years, the drop and bubble profile analysis tensiometry has been developed into a real workhorse for studies in the field of interfacial research [91]. This method is based on the shape of a hanging, sessile or buoyant drop or bubble. The principle is based on the fact that the shape of a liquid meniscus results from the competition of two forces, surface tension and gravity, which is described by the Gauss–Laplace equation. Hence, fitting this equation to experimental drop profile coordinates provides the surface tension $\upgamma$
A principle set-up is shown in Fig. 3. Via a video camera (3) the image of a drop is transferred to a computer (1), digitized and by means of a respective fitting algorithms, the surface tension is accurately determined. Using a precise dosing system (5), the size of a drop or bubble cannot be only kept constant but it allows also to generate harmonic volume changes, which in turn lead to a periodic sinusoidal area compression and expansion. In this way, harmonic surface tension changes are induced, from which the surface viscoelasticity can be determined.
Note, however, the method is applicable only at sufficiently low deformation frequencies, between 0.001 up to about $0.2\mathrm{Hz}$ [88]. For the determination of interfacial rheological parameters at frequencies above $0.2\mathrm{Hz}$ , the profile analysis tensiometry is not suitable and a transfer to small spherical drops/bubbles is required. Then the capillary pressure is to be measured inside the drop/bubble, thus giving again the surface tension as a function of time $\upgamma$ (t) [92]. Such measurements provide dilational rheological parameters of the surface layer for frequencies between 0.1 and $100\mathrm{Hz}$ .
## 4. Experimental results
## 4.1. PSS/DTAB solutions
PSS/DTAB solutions were one of the first systems where twodimensional microheterogeneities of PSC adsorption layers were clearly demonstrated. Monteux et al. discovered a strong increase of the ellipsometric signal at the increase of surfactant concentration in the range close to ${\sf C}_{0}$ and explained this effect by the formation of a PSC microgel in the surface layer [19]. Later two groups measured the dilational surface elasticity of PSS/DTAB solutions by the oscillating drop [38] and oscillating barrier [55] methods in independent studies. The results of the two experimental techniques were somewhat different at low surfactant concentrations probably due to different surface ages and frequencies (cf. also the discussion of these techniques above). However, both methods indicated strong changes of the dynamic surface elasticity in a narrow concentration range near ${\sf C}_{0}$ . The use of the oscillating barrier gave the possibility to measure both components of E as a function of the surface age [55]. At DTAB concentrations much less than ${\sf C}_{0}$ the adsorption film was elastic and the dynamic surface elasticity increased slowly with the surface age and did not reach equilibrium values (higher than $60\mathrm{mN/m}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) during five hours. The comparison of the mean relaxation time with the characteristic diffusion time from the bulk phase to the surface showed that the adsorption kinetics was not controlled by diffusion. The surface viscoelasticity in this concentration range was similar to that of pure concentrated PSS solutions where the surface properties indicated the importance of hydrophobic interactions between the segments [93]. The results on surface visco-elasticity [55] with the account of the earlier data on DTAB surface concentration in the PSS/ DTAB adsorption layer [18] and the layer thickness [18,19] led to the conclusion that the hydrophobic nature of the polystyrene backbone influenced strongly the dynamic surface properties of PSS/DTAB solutions and resulted in a modification of the classic model of PSC adsorption layer where only electrostatic interactions were taken into account (Fig. 4) [1,3,4]. The role of ion pair formation between the surfactant cation and the sulfonate anion is mainly limited to the local neutralization of free charges at the polymer chain. The hydrophobic effect leads not only to the surfactant adsorption but also to the attraction between segments of the polystyrene backbone and hydrocarbon tails of the surfactant, between the segments themselves, and probably between the surfactant tails. As a result the adsorbed film may contain small regions of low effective charge and with high hydrophobic interactions between the components (Fig. 5). These almost two-dimensional aggregates can contain segments of different PSS chains. The charge of PSS trains between the aggregates is not completely compensated. They are rigid and repulse each other as though they do in the system without surfactant. The whole adsorption film is rigid due to the entanglements of different polyelectrolyte chains and strong cohesion between hydrophobic entities inside the two-dimensional aggregates. This system must be characterized by a high modulus of the surface dilational elasticity. On the other hand, the entanglements between different polymer chains can lead to long relaxation times. Probably a similar structure also exists in the adsorption layers of pure PSS if the solution's ionic strength is high enough [93].
At DTAB concentrations a little less than ${\sf C}_{0}$ the dynamic surface elasticity dropped abruptly by almost an order of magnitude indicating the formation of a loose surface structure (Fig. 6). Simultaneously the equilibration rate became faster and the equilibrium values of surface properties were reached within an hour after surface formation. One can connect these strong changes with the formation of microparticles [55] (microgel according to Ref. [19]). The surface elasticity dropped exactly in the range where Monteux et al. observed the onset of the ellipticity increase and at slightly lower concentrations than those corresponding to the microgel formation in the bulk phase [19]. The rigid surface structure depicted in Fig. 5 collapsed in this range. One can describe these structural changes in the surface layer as a transition from an almost two-dimensional and slightly inhomogeneous adsorption layer to a surface film consisting of thicker microparticles with mobile connections between them.
At further concentration increase the dynamic surface elasticity changed only slightly, unlike the ellipticity [19] and the neutron reflectivity profile [18]. This means that the dynamic surface elasticity is not sensitive enough to changes of the number of microparticles in the surface layer which were discovered by other methods. In this concentration range measurements of the surface dilational viscoelasticity give too scarce information on the organization of the adsorbed PSS/DTAB film. Low values of the surface elasticity indicate only a looser packing of the components than at low concentrations.
The PSS chains are not stretched even between the microparticles and contained fewer ionized groups. This picture is in agreement with the faster mechanical relaxation in the adsorbed film. Measurements of the frequency dependence of the dynamic surface elasticity show that the main relaxation time was comparable with the oscillation period, i.e. is of the order of $10s$ [55]. In the concentration range of micellar solutions the dynamic surface elasticity of PSS/DTAB solutions is close to zero. In this case the relaxation of surface stresses takes place at the expense of DTAB monomer exchange between the adsorption layer and the bulk phase.
Recent studies of adsorbed PSS/DTAB layers by scanning atomic force microscopy (AFM) confirmed the main conclusions of Ref. [55]. The PSC adsorption layers were carefully transferred from the solution surface onto freshly cleaved mica plates by the Langmuir–Blodgett method and dried after that at room temperature [56]. The solution contained $0.01\mathrm{wt}.\%$ PSS and the surfactant concentration changed from 0.1 to $20\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathsf{C}_{0}{\approx}0.485\:\mathrm{mM}$ ). Fig. 7 shows a typical example of AFM images at low DTAB concentration $(<0.3\mathrm{mM})$ . In this range the films have numerous regions of low density (holes) and can be approximately described as a two-dimensional gas emulsion. Some small denser regions in the film can be described as two-dimensional aggregates. The film becomes denser with increasing DTAB concentration and the number and size of the holes decrease but the film thickness does not change. This alteration of the film morphology also agrees with the conclusions following from the data on the dynamic surface elasticity [55]. At DTAB concentrations higher than the narrow range corresponding to the abrupt drop of the dynamic surface elasticity $(>0.4\mathrm{mM})$ the film morphology changes abruptly. One can see in Fig. 8 numerous bright regions in the film with a diameter of about $50\mathrm{nm}$ and a thickness of $2{-}2.5\:\mathrm{nm}$ , which exceed the thickness of the surrounding film. These regions can be interpreted as dense aggregates formed in the surface layer when the surface pressure reaches a critical value. The appearance of three-dimensional heterogeneities in the adsorption layer is the result of the collapse of the quasi two-dimensional layer existing at lower surface pressures (Fig. 7), which is also not entirely homogeneous. This results in abrupt changes of the system's surface properties. Fig. 8 shows that nanoaggregates in the surface layer are disconnected. There is a thinner and probably less dense film between them which must be characterized by the lower surface elasticity. Besides, the relaxation of mechanical stresses in the adsorption layer can occur at the expense of the mass exchange between the aggregates and the surrounding film. As a result the aggregate formation leads to the abrupt drop of the dynamic surface elasticity. The further increase of the surfactant concentration results in an increased number of nanoaggregates at the solution surface and also in changes of their shape and size. The increase of the surface area occupied by relatively rigid aggregates has to lead to the increase of the global dynamic surface elasticity according to Eq. (12) but this effect is probably less important as compared with the changes in the continuous twodimensional dispersion phase. In the range of micellar DTAB solutions the surface aggregates do not disappear completely but their shape deviates stronger from a circle and becomes more chaotic [56].
## 4.2. PAMPS-PAM/DTAB and PAMPS-PNIPAM/DTAB solutions
Bhattacharya et al. [54] and Ritacco et al. [40,94] measured the complex dilational surface elasticity of mixed solutions of a copolymer of sodium acrylamido methyl propane sulfonate with acrylamide (PAMPS-PAM) and DTAB by the capillary wave method at frequencies between 200 and $800\mathrm{Hz}$ [54] and by the oscillating bubble method at a frequency of $0.05\mathrm{Hz}$ at two different ratios of AMPS monomers in the copolymer of $10\%$ and $25\%$ , respectively. Significant differences between the results of the two methods are probably mainly connected with strongly non-linear visco-elastic behavior of PAMPS-PAM/DTAB adsorption layers [11]. The Brewster angle microscopy showed that the layer thickness became inhomogeneous after surface compression and approached again the initial homogeneous state only after many hours [40]. The kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity were not reported. This quantity changed smoother with the increase of surfactant concentration as compared with PSS/DTAB solutions and its modulus was lower, below $50\mathrm{mN/m}$ for the complexes with PAMPS-PAM25 and even less for those with PAMPS-PAM10. Similar results were also obtained for solutions of PAMPS-PAM with cetyltrimethylammonium bromide [32].
The different surface visco-elastic behavior of PAMPS-PAM/DTAB and PSS/DTAB solutions can be explained by distinctions in the properties of the polymer chains. The main differences between PSS and PAMPS-PAM consist in the distinct extent of hydrophobicity and charge density of the chain. PAMPS-PAM copolymers studied so far belong to the group of weakly charged polymers, i.e. the distance between two charges in the chain is larger than the Bjerrum length and the counterion condensation is negligible according to Manning's theory of polyelectrolyte solutions [95]. The opposite is true for PSS where one can expect partial condensation of counterions onto the polymer chain. The different hydrophobicity of the two polyelectrolytes causes the difference in the surface properties of their aqueous solutions [93,96]. While the dynamic surface elasticity of concentrated PSS solutions reaches high values ( $\left|\mathrm{E}\right|{\approx}80\mathrm{mN/m})$ and changes only a little with the further increase of ionic strength [93], the surface elasticity of PAMPS-PAM solutions is lower ( $\left|\mathrm{E}\right|{<}40\mathrm{mN}/$ m) and decreases significantly upon addition of sodium chloride [96]. The kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity of PAMPSPAM solutions in $1\mathrm{M}$ sodium chloride have a local maximum like the corresponding dependencies for solutions of non-ionic polymers [70]. The difference in the properties of polyelectrolytes results in the distinctions of the visco-elastic behavior of adsorbed layers of their complexes with the oppositely charged surfactant. The lower dynamic surface elasticity of PAMPS-PAM/DTAB solutions means a looser surface structure as compared with PSS/DTAB solutions. In the later case the adsorption layer thickness is small $({\sim}2\ \mathrm{nm})$ and does not change in a broad concentration range indicating extended conformations of the polymer chain [27]. At the same time the ellipsometric thickness of PAMPS-PAM adsorption layer is higher, about 8 and $20\mathrm{nm}$ close to the CAC for PAMPS-PAM25/DTAB and PAMPS-PAM10/DTAB adsorption layers, respectively [97]. These data show that the PAMPS-PAM chain is more flexible in the surface layer and thereby can probably form loops and tails in some concentration ranges. The non-monotonous changes of the surface density of the complexes with the increase of surfactant concentration lead to the non-monotonous concentration dependence of the dynamic surface elasticity [54]. These changes are not as abrupt as in the case of PSS/ DTAB solutions and therefore cannot be connected with the formation of microaggregates in the adsorption layer.
The properties of a copolymer of sodium 2-acrylamido-2-methyl1-propansulfonate with N-isopropylarylamide (PAMPS-PNIPAM) (the content of sulfonate groups is $18\%$ ) are intermediate between those of PSS and PAMPS-PAM. The charge density of PAMPS-PNIPAM is close to the value of PAMPS-PAM25. On the other hand, the surface activity of PAMPS-PNIPAM is comparable with that of PSS due to hydrophobic isopropyl groups, thus indicating the close extent of hydrophobicity [96,98]. The surface dilational visco-elasticity of PAMPS-PNIPAM solutions in $1\mathrm{M}\mathrm{NaCl}$ is close to the behavior of non-ionic polymer solutions in agreement with theoretical expectations unlike the case of PSS/DTAB solutions and similar to PAMPS-PAM solutions [96]. The dynamic surface properties of PAMPS-PNIPAM/DTAB solutions have some common features with those of both PSS/DTAB and PAMPSPAM/DTAB solutions but are peculiar on the whole [47].
The kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity of PAMPS-PNIPAM/DTAB solutions proved to be non-monotonous unlike previously studied PSC solutions [47]. A local maximum of this quantity arise at low surfactant concentrations up to $0.0001\mathrm{\mM}$ . At the concentrations higher than about $0.1\mathrm{mM}$ (at a polyelectrolyte concentration of $0.01\mathrm{wt}.\%$ ) the maximum shifts to the surface age inaccessible to the applied oscillating bubble method and one can observe only the surface elasticity decrease during a few minutes after surface formation. Unlike some other PSC solutions (cf. Sections $4.3\textmd{-}$ 4.6 below) a local maximum of the surface elasticity in the kinetic dependencies appears in almost the whole investigated concentration range. Only in the range of micellar DTAB solutions the surface elasticity is low and almost constant $\left(1{-}3\mathrm{mN/m}\right)$ . Although the dynamic surface elasticity of other PSC solutions including those of PSS/DTAB also changes very slowly with the surface age at low surfactant concentrations, the kinetic dependencies are always monotonous in this range [24,49–52]. This distinction of PAMPSPNIPAM/DTAB solutions probably indicates a peculiar adsorption mechanism of the complexes. The high surface activity of PAMPSPNIPAM and its relatively low charge density show that the adsorption kinetics of PAMPS-PNIPAM/DTAB complexes can be similar to that of non-ionic polymers where the elasticity maximum was also observed at low polymer concentrations [12,71,99]. One can assume that the polymer chains in the adsorbed complexes are stretched along the surface during the initial steps of the adsorption process resulting in the high surface elasticity (up to $60\mathrm{mN/m}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ). Gradually the surface concentration of the complexes increases. Some additional surfactant ions form ion pairs with sulfonate groups of the polyelectrolyte in the surface layer thereby decreasing the effective chain rigidity. This allows the formation of a looser adsorption layer structure with some loops and tails protruding into the bulk phase (formation of the distal region of the surface layer). The surface stresses can be relaxed at the expense of the matter exchange between the proximal and distal regions of the surface layers and the dynamic surface elasticity begins to decrease. However, the flexibility of the adsorbed chain is still lower than in the case of conventional non-ionic polymers because of the remained charged sulfonate groups and the interaction with the surfactant. Therefore, the surface elasticity does not drop to values of $2{-}4\mathrm{mN/m}$ typical for non-ionic polymer solutions but only decreases to about $20\mathrm{mN/m}$ [47].
Another important peculiarity of the dynamic surface properties of PAMPS-PNIPAM/DTAB solutions consists in the abrupt drop of the surface elasticity from about 16 to $4\mathrm{mN/m}$ when the DTAB concentration approaches $\mathsf{C}_{0}$ (Fig. 9). [47]. This feature was not observed for PAMPS-PAM/DTAB solutions. On the other hand, it is similar to the drop of the surface elasticity for PSS/DTAB solutions and can have a similar explanation. One can speculate that the abrupt changes of the dynamic surface elasticity of PAMPS-PNIPAM/DTAB solutions are also caused by the formation of microparticles in the surface layer leading to a less rigid surface structure. Unlike the case of PSS/DTAB solutions this assumption has not been examined yet by independent experimental methods.
## 4.3. PDADMAC/SDS solutions
The interest in mixtures of PDADMAC and SDS is mainly caused by their ability to form nanoparticles of adjustable size in aqueous solutions [100,101]. The surface properties of these solutions have attracted much attention recently in connection with the nonmonotonous concentration dependence of the surface tension [21–23,25,29,30,46] (cf. the discussion of the “cliff edge peak” in the introductory section). The dilational surface visco-elasticity of PDADMAC/SDS solutions was studied in Ref. [24] by three experimental methods at a polymer concentration of $0.01\mathrm{wt}.\%$ and proved to be very similar to the behavior of the PSS/DTAB system in spite of another sign of the polyelectrolyte charge and its lower surface activity as compared to PSS [94].
The cooperative aggregate formation in PDADMAC/SDS solutions at the polymer concentration of $0.01\mathrm{wt}.\%$ begins at the surfactant concentration of $0.0015\mathrm{mM}$ according to the potentiometric method [102]. The fluorescence spectrometry gives a higher value of $\mathsf{C A C}\approx0.005\mathrm{mM}$ at the polymer concentration of $0.1\mathrm{wt}.\%$ [101]. The surface tension measurements show that PDADMAC/SDS complexes are already present in the surface layer when there is no co-operative binding of the surfactant by the polymer in the bulk phase [24] as it is the case for some other PSC solutions [32,33,47,94]. Plotting the surface tension as a function of the surfactant concentration leads to the “cliff edge peak” if the surface age does not exceed $30{,}000{\mathrm{~s}}.$ . Fig. 10 shows that this is only a consequence of complicated kinetic dependencies of the surface tension and the local maximum of the surface tension concentration dependency disappears at a surface age above about 35,000 s. The non-monotonous changes of the rate of the surface tension decrease (Fig. 10) are probably caused by the microaggregate formation in the surface layer at some concentrations (cf. below).
At low surfactant concentrations $\mathrm{\Omega}\backslash\le0.3\mathrm{mM}$ ) the real part of the dynamic surface elasticity of PDADMAC/SDS solutions exceeds the imaginary part by more than one order of magnitude and the adsorption layer is almost purely elastic [24]. The surface elasticity increases very slowly in this range approaching high values up to $140\mathrm{mN/m}$ if the oscillating barrier method is applied (Fig. 11). The oscillating drop method leads to nearly the same data for most of the surfactant concentrations with the exception of the range of the surface elasticity maximum. The similarity with the corresponding results for PSS/SDS solutions indicates a similar structure of the adsorption film at low concentrations. In this case hydrophobic interactions contribute to the formation of a rigid and probably inhomogeneous surface structure with small regions of high surface density (two-dimensional microaggregates). For both systems, PSS/ DTAB and PDADMAC/SDS, this more or less two-dimensional structure suddenly breaks when the surfactant concentration approaches ${\sf C}_{0}$ and the dynamic surface elasticity drops abruptly to low values (cf. Figs. 6 and 11). One can assume that the increase of the surfactant concentration beyond a critical value promotes the fast growth of three dimensional aggregates in the adsorption layer. At higher concentrations the PDADMAC/SDS adsorption layer contains these aggregates and is characterized by a looser packing of the components. The polyelectrolyte chains are not stretched even between the aggregates and contain fewer ionized groups. Such a structure corresponds to a relatively low dynamic surface elasticity.
In PSS/DTAB solutions aggregation leads to the formation of microparticles and an increase in the adsorption layer thickness. As a result the ellipticity of the solution-air interface increases strongly [19]. However, the ellipsometric thickness of PDADMAC/SDS adsorption layers remains almost the same during a few hours after surface formation at all surfactant concentrations studied. The fast growth of the ellipsometric signal begins only at longer surface life times thus indicating different kinetics of the microparticle formation [24]. The delay time required for the observation of strong changes in the effective ellipsometric thickness decreases with increasing SDS concentration from about $17\mathrm{h}$ in the range just beyond the surface elasticity drop to $6\mathrm{h}$ close to the CMC for DTAB solutions. The process of nucleation is probably very slow in this case and only when the number or size of nuclei has reached a critical value, microparticles can grow fast and begin to influence the ellipsometric signal. Although during the first slow step of particle formation the number and/or size of the nuclei are small and the ellipsometric thickness remains almost constant, the low dynamic surface elasticity points at a loose surface structure. It has to be quite different from a rigid one assumed for lower concentrations, and the adsorption layer has to contain some precursors of microparticles.
The adsorbed amount measured by ellipsometry reaches more than half of its equilibrium value during the first step of adsorption within a minute after the surface creation. In this case the adsorption kinetics is controlled by the polymer and surfactant diffusion from the bulk phase to the surface [24]. The analysis of the subsequent slow adsorption steps must take into account the microheterogeneities in the bulk solution. Dynamic light scattering shows that aggregates with a diameter of about $100\mathrm{nm}$ exist at all investigated surfactant concentrations but the mean size of aggregates increases abruptly only in a narrow concentration range close to ${\sf C}_{0}$ [24]. The particle formation in the bulk phase does not influence the ellipsometric data obtained at surfactant concentrations below ${\sf C}_{0}$ . The particles have an essential residual charge in this range and are almost surface-inactive. Close to ${\sf C}_{0}$ a sharp decrease of the particle charge is accompanied by a drastic decrease of their colloidal stability. In this case the particles coagulate fast and one can observe macroscopic flocks undergoing sedimentation in the gravitational field. These results lead to the assumption that the microparticles do not adsorb at the liquid surface in the whole range of their existence with a possible exception at the isoelectric point.
At the same time the assumed aggregate formation inside the adsorption layer influences the adsorption kinetics and results in a drastic decrease of the dynamic surface elasticity of PDADMAC/SDS solutions close to ${\sf C}_{0}$ . There is also another effect of microparticles on the dynamic surface properties. One can observe strong distortions of the sinusoidal shape of the kinetic surface tension curve measured by the oscillating drop method in the narrow concentration range corresponding to the flock formation in the bulk phase. The surface tension oscillations prove to be purely harmonic at most of the surfactant concentrations while in this range one can observe a nonlinear response of the system to the surface area perturbations. The surface tension decreases upon compression down to about $36.4\mathrm{mN/m}$ and remains almost constant thereafter. Only when the surface expansion starts it begins to increase again [24]. The distortions of sinusoidal oscillations become more pronounced with the decrease of surface tension deviations from this critical value (Fig. 12). Simultaneously the ratio $\mathrm{E_{i}/E_{r}}$ increases and the adsorption
$$
\mathsf{M}\cap\bigcup_{2}\cap\M\cap\dotsc
$$
layer becomes visco-elastic with a characteristic time of the main relaxation process close to the period of drop oscillations $(\sim100s)$ . One can assume that the relaxation process consists in the matter exchange between the adsorption layer and the three-dimensional particles at the surface. These can be the flocks penetrating into the liquid surface as a result of sedimentation to the drop bottom and touching the interface at some sites, the microparticles adsorbed from the bulk phase, which have almost zero charge in a narrow concentration range close to the isoelectric point, or the aggregates formed directly in the surface layer. The more detailed analysis performed for solutions of the complexes between polyacrylic acid (PAA) and DTAB where similar effects were observed corroborates the third mechanism [50,51] (Section 4.5). Some similarities in the surface visco-elastic behavior between PAA/DTAB and PDADMAC/SDS solutions allow extrapolation of this conclusion to the latter system.
One can assume that the particles in the surface layer can easily absorb the individual polyelectrolyte/surfactant complexes upon drop contraction but the reverse transition is limited due to their structure. Only those complexes just absorbed can transit to the surface layer. The dynamic surface tension dependencies give an additional support of the proposed relaxation mechanism. The rate of surface tension decrease accelerates strongly in the range of flock formation. Particles reaching the liquid–gas interface can probably partly dissolve and/or spread along the surface, thus leading to the fast surface tension drop.
At high surfactant concentrations $(>5~\mathrm{mM})$ the PDADMAC/SDS adsorption layer thickness measured by ellipsometry increases significantly, probably due to the formation of multilayered structures. However, this does not lead to appreciable changes in the dynamic surface elasticity, which approaches the values characteristic of pure surfactant solutions (Fig. 11). The surface elasticity like the surface tension is determined mainly by the local concentration in the molecular layer closest to the air phase. The second and subsequent layers can influence the dynamic surface properties only if there is the matter exchange between different layers (cf. Section 2).
## 4.4. PVPCl/SDS solutions
PVPCl is a weak polyelectrolyte insoluble in water at high pH. Therefore all measurements of dynamic surface properties of PVPCl/ SDS solutions were performed at $\mathsf{p H}2$ when the polyelectrolyte dissociation degree approaches unity [49]. This low pH ensures a high ionic strength of the solutions even at low surfactant concentrations and thereby leads to a strong decrease of the electric barrier for the adsorption of PVPCl molecules and their complexes at the solution–air interface. As a result the changes of the surface properties of PVPCl/ SDS solutions are much faster than for other PSC solutions under consideration in this work. If the surfactant concentration exceeds $0.01\mathrm{mM}$ , the surface properties reach equilibrium values within a few minutes after surface creation.
Solutions containing 0.01 wt. $\%$ of PVPCl become opalescent at SDS concentrations above $0.02\mathrm{mM}$ and one can observe the formation of macroscopic flocks in a narrow concentration range close to $0.75\mathrm{mM}$ . The macroscopic heterogeneity of the solution leads to a peculiar surface tension kinetic dependence at this concentration [49]. The surface tension drops in less than one minute after surface creation to about $25\mathrm{mN/m}$ due to the adsorption of the complexes of high surface activity and increases thereafter over few hours up to almost
$60\mathrm{mN/m}$ . The latter step is obviously a consequence of the gradual growth of large visible particles. They absorb polyelectrolyte and surfactant from the solution leading to the gradual depletion of the interface and thereby to the slow surface tension increase. This peculiarity of the surface tension kinetic dependence results in the “cliff edge peak” if one measures the surface tension as a function of surfactant concentration more than one hour after surface creation [27,49]. Macroscopic particles are not formed and the concentration dependence of the surface tension becomes monotonous for PVPCl/ SDS solutions in $0.1\mathrm{~M~NaCl}$ (PVPCl/SDS/NaCl(0.1 M)).
The real part of the dynamic surface elasticity increases monotonously as a function of surface age for most of the investigated solutions [49]. The only exception is a narrow concentration range corresponding to the “cliff edge peak” of the surface tension isotherm when large flocks are formed in the solution. In this case the surface elasticity increases only during the first adsorption step and begins to decrease in the course of large aggregate formation.
The concentration dependencies of dynamic surface elasticity for both PVPCl/SDS and PVPCl/SDS/NaCl(0.1 M) (Fig. 13) solutions resemble those of PSS/DTAB and PDADMAC/SDS systems [24,55]. The real part of the dynamic surface elasticity of the former two systems is high at low concentrations (up to $140\mathrm{mN/m}$ for PVPCl/ SDSD and $240\mathrm{mN/m}$ PVPCl/SDS/NaCl(0.1 M) solutions), drops by a few times close to the SDS concentration of $0.01\mathrm{mM}$ and decreases further when approaching the CMC of the SDS solutions. The observed similarity with the previously studied PSC solutions implies that the drastic changes of the surface elasticity in a narrow concentration range are also caused by a transition from an almost two-dimensional rigid surface structure to the adsorption film containing nano- and microaggregates. The microparticle formation in the surface layer is not a result of only electrostatic forces between polyelectrolyte segments and oppositely charged surfactant ions. One has to take into account also the hydrophobic forces to explain the main features of the surface visco-elasticity. This is especially evident for PVPCl/SDS solutions where, unlike other PSC solutions, the concentration corresponding to the surface elasticity drop is more than thirty times lower than ${\sf C}_{0}$ . Note that the difference was only about $20\%$ for PDADMAC/SDS and PSS/DTAB solutions [24,55]. Dynamic light scattering does not see any changes in the size of the microaggregates in the bulk of PVPCl/SDS solutions at concentrations corresponding to the surface elasticity drop [49]. This observation supports additionally the conclusion that the microparticles are formed directly in the surface layer and not adsorbed from the bulk phase.
An indirect proof of microparticle formation at the surface is the appearance of higher harmonics of surface tension oscillations, which are not purely sinusoidal in the range of surfactant concentrations from $0.02\mathrm{mM}$ to $1\mathrm{mM}$ for PVPCl/SDS solutions. The non-symmetrical surface tension oscillations (Fig. 12) arise if the system contains microparticles close to the surface, for example, due to the matter exchange between the particles and the surrounding adsorption layer.
Another important distinction of the dynamic surface properties of PVPCl/SDS solutions from those of other studied systems consists in the high value of the dynamic surface elasticity at maximum. To the best of our knowledge this value $({\sim}240\ \mathrm{mN/m})$ is the highest among those ever reported in literature on surface rheology of surfactant solutions. It drops to about $110\mathrm{mN/m}$ for the solutions in $0.1\mathrm{MNaCl}$ One can speculate that the hydrophobic interactions lead to the entanglements of the neighboring polyelectrolyte chains and the formation of a rigid surface structure. At the same time the repulsion between neighboring charged groups of polyelectrolyte molecules leads to an effective rigidity of the polyelectrolyte chains at low ionic strength thereby contributing to the high dynamic surface elasticity. The addition of sodium chloride screens the electrostatic repulsion between polyelectrolyte segments of the same charge and results in a surface elasticity decrease. However, the ionic strength is not low even in solutions without NaCl. Besides, these simple ideas are insufficient to explain strong differences between the absolute values of the dynamic surface elasticity in the range of the maximum for various PSC solutions. It is necessary to take into account that the peculiarities in the concentration dependence of surface elasticity, first of all the high absolute values, are probably determined by the macroscopic or mesoscopic structure of the adsorption layer, which in turn depends on the characteristics of two-dimensional or threedimensional polyelectrolyte/surfactant aggregates. Therefore the relation between the chemical structure of polyelectrolyte and surfactant molecules and the measured surface properties is indirect and can hardly be predicted in the framework of the existing simple models.
The main distinction of the dynamic surface properties of PVPCl/ SDS solutions consists probably in the high imaginary part of the dynamic surface elasticity. The adsorption layers of PVPCl/SDS solutions with and without NaCl are both visco-elastic at a frequency of $0.14\mathrm{Hz}$ and concentrations above $0.01\mathrm{mM}$ . The imaginary part of the dynamic surface elasticity can sometimes exceed its real part indicating that the relaxation processes with a characteristic time of the order of $10s$ proceed in the adsorption layer. As a result, both components of the dynamic surface elasticity depend strongly on frequency in the concentration range from 0.01 to $1\mathrm{mM}$ . The adsorption layer is purely elastic only at lower concentrations where the real part of the complex surface elasticity is much higher than the imaginary part.
An estimation of the main relaxation time $\boldsymbol{\uptau}$ follows from the rheological Maxwell model Eq. (10)
$$
\boldsymbol{\uptau}=\frac{E_{r}}{\upomega E_{i}}.
$$
The calculated relaxation time for PVPCl/SDS solutions in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ NaCl decreases by more than one order of magnitude in a narrow concentration range from 0.005 to $0.02\mathrm{mM}$ [49]. In the concentration range from 0.02 to $1\mathrm{mM}$ the relaxation time almost coincides with the period of surface area oscillations and at higher concentrations the imaginary part of the dynamic surface elasticity is close to the error limits and calculations become impossible. A similar behavior was discovered also for solutions without NaCl.
The calculated relaxation time is close to the characteristic time of the surfactant diffusion from the bulk phase to the surface at surfactant concentrations above $0.2\mathrm{mM}$ . Therefore one can explain the observed relaxation of surface stresses under the assumption that the PSC complexes in the surface layer lose gradually their stability with increasing surfactant concentration from 0.005 to $0.2\mathrm{mM}$ . In this case the number of surfactant molecules available for the exchange with the bulk phase also increases resulting in a decrease of the main relaxation time. However, the comparison with results for other PSC solutions shows that the more probable relaxation mechanism consists in the matter exchange between the microaggregates and the surrounding adsorption layer. The number and size of aggregates increase fast with the surfactant concentrations leading to strong changes of the main relaxation time in a narrow concentration range. This time is governed by the chemical nature of the polymer chain and differs strongly for the studied PSC solutions.
## 4.5. PAA/DTAB and PMA/DTAB solutions
The dynamic surface properties of PAA/DTAB solutions were measured at a constant polyelectrolyte concentration of $0.53\mathrm{mM}$ and $\mathsf{p H}9.2$ where the dissociation degree is close to the maximal possible value [50]. Unlike PVPCl/SDS solutions, the dynamic surface properties of PAA/DTAB solutions change slowly with surface age and do not reach equilibrium values within five hours after surface creation for surfactant concentration below $1\mathrm{mM}$ . The kinetic dependencies of the surface tension are monotonous in the whole investigated concentration range. The surface tension as a function of surfactant concentration displays two specific points typical for most of the PSC solutions. After a fast drop at low surfactant concentrations $(\sim0.15\mathrm{mM})$ ) the surface tension reaches a plateau. The beginning of the plateau is sometimes associated with the CAC [1,3,4,8]. The end of the plateau at the surfactant concentration of around $2\mathrm{mM}$ can be attributed to the saturation of the polyelectrolyte chains by surfactant molecules and the onset of the increase of free surfactant in the solution bulk [1,3,8]. Similar results were also obtained for solutions of polymethacrylic acid/DTAB (PMA/DTAB) at $\mathsf{p H9.2}$ .
The concentration dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity of PAA/DTAB (Fig. 14) and PMA/DTAB solutions are typical for PSC systems and characterized by a fast drop in a narrow concentration range. However, the kinetic dependencies of this quantity are sometimes more complicated than those for other systems and can have one or two local maxima [50].
One can divide the whole surfactant concentration range into four main zones according to distinctive features of the kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity (Fig. 14). In zone 1 at DTAB concentrations below about $0.15\mathrm{mM}$ the dynamic surface elasticity of PAA/DTAB and PMA/DTAB solutions increases monotonously to high values $({\sim}70~\mathrm{mN/m})$ indicating the formation of a rigid adsorption layer structure, which is characteristic to other polyelectrolyte/surfactant solutions in this concentration range. Unlike PVPCl/SDS solutions, the increase of the ionic strength of PAA/DTAB solutions by the addition of $0.1\mathrm{~M~NaCl}$ results in a strong drop of the dynamic surface elasticity to low values $({<}9\mathrm{mN}/\mathrm{m})$ which are typical for solutions of non-ionic polymers [12,71,99]. The difference between the two systems is probably caused by the different contribution of hydrophobic interactions in the formation of the rigid surface structure. The shielding of electrostatic interactions by NaCl and the corresponding decrease of the effective rigidity of the polymer chain has a weaker influence on the dilational surface visco-elasticity of PVPCl/SDS solutions.
In zone 2 in the DTAB concentration range from 0.15 up to $2\mathrm{mM}$ the adsorption becomes faster and the surface properties almost approach equilibrium values during five hours after surface creation. At the same time the kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity become non-monotonous for solutions containing both polyacids (Fig. 15). Note that one can observe these peculiarities in homogeneous solutions and therefore they must have another explanation than for heterogeneous PVPCl/SDS solutions. Unlike the case of PAMPS-PNIPAM/DTAB solutions, the local maxima for PAA/ DTAB and PMA/DTAB solutions arise only if the surfactant concentration is high enough $(>0.15\mathrm{mN/m})$ and cannot be explained by the formation of the distal region of the surface layer (cf. Section 4.2).
The interpretation of the experimental results becomes easier if one takes into account the distortion of the harmonic surface tension oscillations, which arises simultaneously with the local maxima in the kinetic dependencies $_{.\mathrm{c}>0.15\mathrm{mM}}$ for both systems). The surface tension oscillations in zone 1 and during the initial rise of the surface elasticity for more concentrated solutions are almost sinusoidal indicating that the system is in the linear domain. This behavior breaks abruptly when the surface elasticity reaches the maximum value (Fig. 16). Although the surface area oscillations are sinusoidal, the corresponding surface tension oscillations contain a significant contribution of higher harmonics and the system becomes non-linear in the time range beyond the local surface elasticity maximum. Fig. 15 shows the consecutive changes of the time dependence of surface tension in the course of surface oscillations. Fig. 17 shows the corresponding changes of the total harmonic distortion (THD), which characterizes the deviation from pure sinusoidal oscillations [50].
To calculate the THD the experimental surface tension oscillations have to be processed according to the following algorithm [103]
$$
\mathsf{R e}\{G(f_{n})\}\approx\frac{2\Delta}{Z_{c y c l}T}\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}g_{k}\ c o s(2\uppi f_{n}t_{k})
$$
$$
\mathrm{Im}\{G(f_{n})\}\approx\frac{2\Delta}{Z_{c y c l}T}\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}g_{k}\ s i n(2\uppi f_{n}t_{k}).
$$
Here, the summation is performed over all points of all cycles included in a selected time interval. $\mathbb{R e}\{G(f_{n})\}$ and $\operatorname{Im}\{G(f_{n})\}\mathrm{are}$ the real and the imaginary part of the complex surface tension, G, of frequency, $f_{n}$ . Moreover, $g_{k}$ is the experimental value of surface tension at time $t_{k},\Delta$ is the time interval between two consecutive points, $Z_{c y c l}$ is the number of cycles in a selected time interval, $T/2$ is half the period.
The THD-parameter can be calculated by the following equation
$$
T H D=\frac{{\left(a_{2}^{2}+a_{3}^{2}+...+a_{n}^{2}\right)}^{1/2}}{a_{1}}
$$
where $a_{1}$ is the amplitude value at the fundamental frequency and $a_{2},a_{3},...,a_{n}$ are the amplitude values of the higher harmonics $\mathbf{\partial}_{a_{n}}=$ $[\mathrm{Re}\{G(f_{n})\}^{2}+\mathrm{Im}\{G(f_{n})\}^{2}]^{1/2})$ . The number $n$ of higher harmonics is chosen in connection with the specific system under examination. The THD-values are usually determined up to the fifth harmonic component (i.e., $n=5$ ). In Fig. 17 the THD-values are expressed as a percentage of the fundamental frequency amplitude according to the usual practice.
The transparency of polyacid/DTAB solutions at concentrations corresponding to the non-linear visco-elastic behavior of the adsorption film excludes the influence of large aggregates on the surface tension oscillations and the observed effects have to be induced by microparticles at the surface. The strong drop of the dynamic surface elasticity to lower values with increasing surfactant concentration corresponds exactly to the beginning of distortions of surface tension oscillations. The surface tension decrease upon compression to a critical value (44 and $47.5\mathrm{mN/m}$ for $\mathrm{PAA}/\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{TAB}$ and $\mathrm{PMA}/\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{TAB}$ solutions, respectively) results in the formation of aggregates in the adsorption layer (Fig. 18). One can assume that the local surface concentration of the adsorbed complexes does not change at further compression as a result of the mass exchange between the aggregates at the surface and an almost two-dimensional adsorption layer around them. The size and/or the number of aggregates increase upon compression but the local surface concentration and hence the surface tension remains almost constant. The adsorption layer expansion leads to a mass transfer in the opposite direction and the surface tension does not change until the disappearance of aggregates or until their internal structure hinders their dissolution. In this case the surface tension begins to increase. The adsorption layer becomes visco-elastic in zone 2 and this gives additional evidence of relaxation processes with a rate comparable with that of surface expansion and compression.
In zone 3 at surfactant concentrations from 1.5 to $15\mathrm{mM}$ the kinetic dependencies of surface tension display a plateau after a strong initial drop. Beyond the plateau range the surface tension decreases again approaching equilibrium values. The width of the plateau decreases with increasing surfactant concentration. The dynamic surface elasticity as a function of surface age displays two local maxima (Fig. 19) corresponding approximately to the beginning and the end of the surface tension plateaus.
The first maximum of the dynamic surface elasticity arises when the surface tension reaches ${\sim}44~\mathrm{mN/m}$ for PAA/DTAB solutions and ${\sim}47.5$ for PMA/DTAB solutions, respectively, and is accompanied by the appearance of non-harmonic surface tension oscillations. This means that it has the same nature as the single maximum in zone 2 and is connected with the microparticle formation when the surface tension reaches the critical value. The subsequent regions of approximately constant surface tension and dynamic surface elasticity correspond to a microheterogeneous adsorption layer with a number of aggregates. One can assume that the adsorption of polyelectrolyte/surfactant complexes continues further leading to the increase of the number and/or size of the aggregates at the surface. The aggregates begin to interact and the dynamic surface elasticity increases again during the next step of adsorption. Simultaneously the surface tension oscillations begin to approach a more harmonic form. Zhang et al. using neutron reflectivity discovered a multilayer structure at the surface of PAA/DTAB solutions in the concentration range corresponding to zone 3 [104]. These findings allow interpretation of the second maximum in the kinetic dependencies of surface elasticity. Some surface aggregates in zone 3 can be displaced into the sublayer with increasing surface concentration (Fig. 18). In this case a mass exchange between different layers becomes possible and the dynamic surface elasticity decreases again. The simultaneous conformational transitions in the proximal region of the surface layer lead to a further surface tension decrease. Both maxima are shifted to lower surface ages with increasing surfactant concentration and at concentrations above about $5\mathrm{mM}$ the applied experimental method allows a detection of the second local maximum only. The difference between the surface elasticity values of PMA/DTAB and PAA/DTAB in zone 3 is probably caused by the different extent of hydrophobicity of the two polyacids and consequently by the different interactions between their segments and the surfactant tails [50].
In zone 4 the bulk surfactant concentration exceeds the CMC and the surface properties of mixed PAA/DTAB and PMA/DTAB solutions are determined by the excess of DTAB molecules in the surface layer. In this case the dynamic surface elasticity approaches zero at the applied frequencies and the surface tension coincides with the value for surfactant micellar solutions.
## 4.6. $P A A/C_{n}T A B$ solutions
The oscillating ring method was applied to solutions of complexes between PAA and different $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{n}}\mathrm{TAB}$ at $\mathsf{p H}\ 9.2$ and a polymer concentration of $0.53\mathrm{mM}$ with the aim to elucidate the influence of hydrophobic interactions on the dilational surface rheological properties of these systems [51].
The kinetic and concentration dependencies of the surface tension are close to the data for PAA/DTAB solutions for $\mathrm{n}=10$ , 14. The results for $\mathrm{PAA/C_{8}T A B}$ solutions are shifted appreciably to higher surfactant concentrations due to the lower surface activity of the complex. The concentration dependence of the surface tension of $\mathrm{PAA/C_{16}T A B}$ solutions has a local maximum in the range of precipitation (“cliff edge peak”). For other systems the surfactant hydrophobicity is insufficient to induce a noticeable transition of the components from the surface layer to macroscopic aggregates in the bulk phase. The increase of the surfactant's hydrocarbon chain length leads to a decrease of the surface tension in the plateau region beyond the abrupt surface tension drop. The same effect was also observed for other systems $(\mathrm{PSS}/\mathrm{C_{n}T A B}$ [26], $\mathrm{PAMPS}/\mathrm{C_{n}T A B}$ and carboxymethylcellulose/ $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{n}T A B}$ [105]) and caused by the increase of the surface activity of surfactants and their complexes with PAA at the transition from $\mathtt{n}=8$ to $\mathrm{n}=16$ .
All PAA/ $\mathrm{'C_{n}T A B}$ solutions display almost the same changes of the kinetic dependencies of the surface properties with increasing surfactant concentration and the main distinctions are of quantitative rather than qualitative nature. Therefore, the main conclusions obtained from studies of $\mathrm{PAA}/\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{TAB}$ solutions become also true for solutions of PAA complexes with the other members of the homological series of alkyltrimethylammonium bromides. The whole concentration range can be also divided into four zones which are characterized by different kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity (Section 4.5). The dynamic surface elasticity of all investigated $\mathrm{PAA/C_{n}T A B}$ solutions increase monotonously with surface age in zone 1. In this case the adsorption layer is characterized by the high surface elasticity as a result of the formation of a rigid two-dimensional structure. The rate of changes of surface properties in zone 2 increases and the surface tension reaches an equilibrium value during the time of measurements. In this concentration range the surface tension oscillations induced by periodic expansions (compressions) of the surface area become non-harmonic and the kinetic dependencies of the surface elasticity are non-monotonous. The surface tension decreases during surface layer compression only up to a critical value and remains almost constant after that until the surface expansion starts again. This critical surface tension value corresponds probably to the onset of microaggregate formation in the surface layer. The further surface compression after the beginning of aggregate formation leads only to the increase of their number and/or size but does not change the local surface concentration in the area around the aggregates, which determines the surface tension value. As a result the measured surface elasticity is only an effective value in the second zone and has local maxima if measured as a function of surface age and surfactant concentration. These maxima are less pronounced and shift to higher concentrations for ${\mathsf{C}}_{10}{\mathrm{T}}{\mathsf{A B}},$ /PAA and ${\mathsf{C}}_{8}{\mathrm{T}}{\mathsf{A B}}_{\prime}$ /PAA solutions. The shape of the kinetic curves of surface elasticity and the non-linearity of the surface tension oscillations show that microaggregates are formed directly in the surface layer as result of adsorption layer compression and are not adsorbed from the solution.
The surface tension values of PAA $\mathrm{'C_{8}T A B}$ solutions corresponding to the onset of strong changes in the surface elasticity in the course of adsorption due to aggregate formation decrease with increasing surfactant concentration. This result apparently indicates that the rates of adsorption and aggregate formation are similar. The aggregate content in the surface layer is close to equilibrium for slow adsorption kinetics (low surfactant concentration). When the adsorption kinetics becomes faster than the aggregate formation, a local equilibrium in the surface layer cannot be achieved and a remarkable growth of the number of aggregates can start only at higher surface pressures. This can lead to a shift of the surface elasticity maximum toward higher surface pressures. The critical surface tension value of aggregate formation in $\mathrm{PAA}/\mathrm{C}_{10}\mathrm{TAB}$ adsorption layers depends on the surfactant concentration only in the range of low concentrations. The surface tension values corresponding to the onset of aggregation in the adsorption layers of other $\mathrm{PAA/C_{n}T A B}$ complexes are almost independent of the surfactant concentration and thereby the adsorption rate exceeds the rate of aggregate formation.
The surface tension in zone 3 is almost constant after a fast initial drop and starts decreasing again after a certain time, while the kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity display two local maxima. The first maximum like the maximum in the second zone arises when the surface tension oscillations become non-harmonic and is caused by microaggregate formation in the surface layer. The increase of the number and/or size of the aggregates and the interactions between them during subsequent adsorption steps lead to the growth in dynamic surface elasticity. The further growth of the number of aggregate leads to their displacement into the subphase and the formation of a multilayer structure at the surface. This results in the appearance of a new mechanism of surface stress relaxation and the surface elasticity shows a second local maximum. The surfactant concentrations corresponding to the multilayer formation decrease remarkably with the increase in the surfactant's hydrophobic chain length. This indicates an increase in the surface aggregation concentration or their sizes with increasing surfactant surface activity. The surface tension remains almost unchanged in the time range from the beginning of aggregate formation up to the multilayer structure formation and one can observe a plateau in the kinetic surface tension dependence.
The equilibrium values of the surface properties of $\mathrm{PAA}/\mathrm{C}_{10}\mathrm{TAB}$ and PAA $\mathrm{'C_{8}T A B}$ solutions are not reached during the measurements in the third concentration zone. The surface elasticity of other PAA/ $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{n}}\mathrm{TAB}$ solutions increases in this range with the increase in surfactants' surface activity. Hence, the increase of hydrophobic interactions leads to the formation of a more rigid structure of the adsorption layer. A similar tendency is observed for solutions of the complexes between polyacids and ${\mathsf{C}}_{12}{\mathrm{T}}{\mathsf{A B}}$ when the transition from PAA to the more hydrophobic PMA also leads to an increase of the dynamic surface elasticity in zone 3 (Section 4.5).
The kinetic dependency of the dynamic surface elasticity of PAA/ ${\mathsf{C}}_{16}{\mathrm{T}}{\mathsf{A B}}$ solutions changes significantly at surfactant concentrations above $0.6\mathrm{mM}$ in the range of precipitation. The surface elasticity increases monotonously with the surface age as a result of the structural changes of the polyelectrolyte/surfactant complexes in the bulk solution after precipitation and thereby do not indicate the formation of a multilayer structure at the surface. The kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity of PAA/C16TAB solutions do not display two local maxima and thereby do not indicate the formation of a multilayer structure in the surface layer. The surfactant concentration range corresponding to the multilayer formation is probably beyond the investigated range of $\mathrm{PAA/C_{8}T A B}$ solutions because the decrease of the length of surfactant hydrocarbon chains results in a shift of the observed multilayer structures to higher concentrations.
The surface properties in zone 4 are again determined by the excess of surfactant molecules in the adsorption layer.
## 4.7. PEI/SDS solutions
The main features of the kinetic and concentration dependencies of the dynamic surface properties of alkaline PEI/SDS solutions $\mathrm{\Phi_{pH3.2}}$ and polyelectrolyte concentration of 0.02 wt. $\%$ ) on surfactant concentration are close to the corresponding results for most of the other PSC solutions (Sections 4.1–4.6.) and are not discussed in detail here. The dynamic surface elasticity is high (up to $70\mathrm{mN/m}$ ) at low SDS concentrations and drops to low values close to ${\sf C}_{0}$ [52]. At higher concentrations the kinetic dependencies of the surface elasticity become non-monotonous and one can observe a distortion of the sinusoidal surface tension oscillations. The surface tension does not decrease further if it reaches a critical value of $37\mathrm{mN/m}$ upon surface compression. These data indicate the formation of microaggregates in the adsorption film at the critical surface tension value as for other PSC solutions. In this case the relaxation of surface stresses occurs at the expense of the matter exchange between the aggregates and the surrounding adsorption film. The imaginary part of the surface elasticity approaches the real part and the main relaxation time changes drastically from about $100s$ at the SDS concentration of $0.05\mathrm{mM}$ to $10s$ at $0.2\mathrm{mM}$
The main distinctions from the results for all previously studied PSC solutions can be observed at low surfactant concentrations when the rate of change of surface properties is not a monotonous function of the surface age [52]. At SDS concentrations less than $0.05\mathrm{mM}$ the surface tension is constant and close to the value for pure water during the initial step of adsorption, while the surface elasticity is close to zero. The time, when the surface properties almost do not change (lag-time), decreases with the increase of surfactant concentration. After this period the rate of change of the surface properties begins to increase. The surface tension decreases abruptly to ${\sim}70~\mathrm{mN/m}$ when the surface elasticity increases up to ${\sim}40~\mathrm{mN/m}$ . In the next step of adsorption one can observe only slow changes of the surface properties during several hours. It is noteworthy that the surface tension oscillations become inharmonic (contain a significant contribution of higher harmonics) in the range of abrupt changes of the surface properties. The surface tension is almost constant upon surface compression for a few seconds until a certain moment when it begins to decrease (Fig. 20). Upon surface expansion the opposite behavior is observed: the surface tension increases until a certain moment and becomes almost constant after that. The Fourier analysis of the surface tension oscillations discovers higher harmonics in this case. A part of the period of the oscillations corresponding to the constant surface tension decreases and the amplitude of oscillations increases with increasing surface age. This leads to the increase of the effective surface elasticity. The oscillations become purely harmonic (the contribution of higher harmonics becomes negligible) when the mean surface tension value approaches ${\sim}70~\mathrm{mN/m}$ and the calculated surface elasticity increases to about $40\mathrm{mN/m}$ .
The constancy of surface tension immediately after surface formation was already observed previously for the adsorption of globular proteins from aqueous solutions [106,107] and explained by the peculiarities of the equation of state for protein adsorption layers [108]. The interactions between compact globules at low surface concentrations are negligible and the surface pressure is close to zero. The significant repulsion between globules appears only after a certain critical surface concentration, when the surface tension starts to decrease rapidly. These phenomena are connected with the peculiarities of the tertiary structure of proteins and have never been observed for solutions of complexes between synthetic polyelectrolyte and surfactants besides for PEI/SDS solutions. The main distinctions between the system under consideration in this section and other investigated polyelectrolyte/surfactant solutions consist in the structure of the polyelectrolyte molecules. Unlike other polyelectrolytes PEI is a branched polymer. Apparently a spreading of the polymer coil does not occur during the adsorption of PEI or its complexes with the surfactant. The adsorbed PEI/SDS complexes have a compact structure and thereby they can be considered as a rough physical model of globular proteins. Therefore one can observe a period of constant surface tension at the initial step of adsorption of PEI/SDS complexes. This period decreases with the increase of the surfactant concentration due to the faster approach of the critical surface concentration where the complexes start to mutually interact. The surface elasticity data support this interpretation. The surface elasticity begins to deviate from zero only after a critical surface concentration has reached. When the surface concentration of complexes is close to the critical value, the surface compression leads to the surface tension decrease. At the same time, the surface expansion leads to the increase of surface tension up to the value for water and further changes of the surface tension do not occur. This leads to the appearance of higher harmonics in the measured surface tension oscillations.
## 5. Conclusions and outlook
Studies on the dilational surface rheology of various PSC solutions have been performed during the last decade. Although the studied synthetic polyelectrolytes have different chemical nature and therefore differ in the extent of hydrophobicity, chain topology and flexibility, the surface rheological behavior of the most PSC solutions are characterized by some common features. For example, the dynamic surface elasticity of most of the dilute PSC solutions at surfactant concentrations much less than ${\sf C}_{0}$ and the CAC changes during many hours after surface formation and approaches high values indicating a rigid surface structure. Only the decrease of the polyelectrolyte chain's charge density leads to a different behavior (Section 4.2). The complexes in the bulk phase in the concentration range below the CAC are formed due to ion pair formation between some polyelectrolyte charged groups and the surfactant ions. It is hardly possible that a mere adsorption of these complexes is sufficient to create a rigid structure in the adsorption layer. The adsorption film formation is usually much slower that the diffusion of the components from the bulk phase to the surface [24,55,109]. Another argument in favor of the distinction between the complexes in the bulk solution and in the surface layer stems from the comparison between the surface properties of the solutions of polyelectrolyte/surfactant complexes and polysoaps with the same content and length of alkyl groups. The surface activity of the complexes proved to be much higher and thereby the structure of the corresponding adsorption layers is different [80–82]. This means that the complexes at the surface must contain a larger number of surfactant ions per polyelectrolyte chain than the complexes in the bulk and the distribution of alkyl chains in the former case must favor their contact with the gas phase. The first model of PSC adsorption layers where the surfactant molecules interact electrostatically with the ionized groups of the polyelectrolyte chain and play the role of anchors for the polyelectrolyte chain stretched beneath the surfactant monolayer takes these conditions into account (Fig. 4) [1,16]. However, more recent findings discussed above show that hydrophobic interactions also contribute to the rigidity of the PSC adsorption layer at low concentrations and it has to be heterogeneous at the nano-scale (Fig. 5). The detailed mechanisms of the adsorption layer formation remain to be elucidated.
At a certain surfactant concentration below ${\sf C}_{0}$ the rigid structure breaks and the dynamic surface elasticity drops abruptly to low values. The strong decrease of the surface elasticity was discovered not only for complexes of surfactants with synthetic polyelectrolytes but also with some polysaccharides [83,84]. A strong drop of the surface elasticity also occurs if the surfactant concentration is constant and one changes the polyelectrolyte concentration [86].
The drastic changes of the adsorption layer structure can be connected with the formation of nano- and micro-particles in the surface layer at a certain value of surface pressure. One can trace the process of their formation by studying the non-linear response to sinusoidal oscillations of the surface area. The Fourier analysis of induced oscillations of the surface tension discovers higher harmonics when the adsorption film contains aggregates. The surface tension decreases upon surface compression only if the surface tension does not reach a critical value and is almost constant after that up to the moment of surface expansion. It is possible to assume that the surface tension does not decrease at compression due to the matter exchange between the particles and the surrounding adsorption layer. The ellipsometry gives another proof of the particle formation in the surface layer [19,24]. Unfortunately, most of the applied experimental techniques give only scarce and indirect information on the adsorption layer structure and characteristics of the particles formed. Only the application of AFM allowed the estimation of their size [56]. Meantime, there are some indications that the particles have a regular shape and form periodical structures at the surface and, therefore, the adsorption layers of PSC solutions have some perspectives from the point of view of the formation of nano-particles for various applications.
In spite of some general features, the surface rheological behavior of the systems discussed above has important peculiarities. The rate of adsorption layer formation, the maximal surface elasticity, the relation between the real and imaginary parts of the dynamic surface elasticity, the surfactant concentration corresponding to the elasticity drop, the surface pressure of particle formation at the surface and some other parameters depend strongly on the PSC system. In some cases one can connect these properties with the hydrophobicity of the components but often the relation between the molecular characteristics of the polyelectrolyte and surfactant and the surface rheological properties is not sufficiently clear. These difficulties are mainly caused by poor knowledge of the adsorption layer structure at the nano-level. Nevertheless, the application of the methods of dilation surface rheology to PSC solutions gave a possibility to elucidate the main processes taking place at the air–water interface after its creation. The applicability of these methods to kinetic studies is probably one of their main advantages. The interpretation of non-monotonous kinetic dependencies of the dynamic surface elasticity is usually possible and gives information on the corresponding conformational transitions in the surface layer. This approach proved to be especially useful in studies of protein denaturation at liquid interfaces [110,111].
